```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figure/",
                      fig.width = 6, fig.height = 6, dpi = 96, error = FALSE)
```

[OlmoEarth v1.3](https://allenai.org/papers/olmoearth-v1-3) from the Allen
Institute for AI summarises each year of Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2 and Landsat
8/9 imagery as a 128-dimensional vector per 10 m pixel. The
[embeddings](https://source.coop/olmoearth/olmoearth-v1-3-embeddings) are
public on Source Cooperative as one Zarr v3 store, with no account or
signing needed:

* one group per UTM zone (`utm01` to `utm60`), each holding an
  `embeddings` array with axes `(time, band, y, x)`;
* nine years, 2017 to 2025 (2023 to 2025 are global, earlier years are
  being backfilled);
* Int8 codes, decoded by `sign(q) * (|q| / 127.5)^2`, with `-128` as nodata;
* the first 64 dimensions form a complete embedding on their own
  (Matryoshka training) and are stored in their own chunks.

This vignette reads two years over Jasper National Park, Alberta, where a
wildfire burned about 33,000 ha in July 2024, and maps how far each pixel's
embedding moved between 2023 and 2025.

## Open the store

GDAL's Zarr driver reads the store directly. A `ZARR:` connection string
names the zone group and the array; garry adds the `/vsicurl/` prefix to a
bare `https` store, as it does for any URL.

```{r open, message = FALSE}
library(garry)

store <- "https://data.source.coop/olmoearth/olmoearth-v1-3-embeddings/embeddings.zarr"
src <- paste0('ZARR:"', store, '/utm11":/embeddings')
```

Each zone is in its own UTM CRS (Jasper is in zone 11, `EPSG:32611`), on a
10 m grid anchored at the zone origin. A grid in that CRS at 10 m with edges
on multiples of 10 m is a plain window of the array, so the read needs no
resampling. Edges on multiples of 2560 m also line up with the store's
256-pixel chunks, so every chunk fetched is used in full.

```{r grid}
grid <- grid_spec("EPSG:32611", c(417280, 5834240, 445440, 5870080), res = 10)
dim(grid)
```

`lazy_dataset()` reads the array's dimension tags (GDAL flattens
`(time, band)` into classic bands and labels each one) and lays the array out
as dataset bands by year. `bands = 1:64` keeps the 64-dimension embedding,
which halves the bytes read.

```{r dataset}
oe <- lazy_dataset(src, grid, bands = 1:64)
oe
time_labels(oe)
```

Nothing has been fetched yet beyond the store's metadata.

## Read two years

`time_sel()` picks a year, `dequantize_olmoearth()` decodes the Int8 codes
(the nodata code is already NaN, so it stays NaN), and `stack_bands()` turns
the year into one `(band, y, x)` cube.

```{r years}
year <- function(y) {
  time_sel(oe, y) |>
    lazy_map(fn = dequantize_olmoearth, dtype = "f32") |>
    stack_bands()
}
```

Both years feed every step below, so fetch them once into local cubes.
`materialise()` runs both in one execution. The store's chunks hold 64
bands each, and garry reads all 64 bands of a window in one task, so every
chunk crosses the network once.

```{r materialise, message = FALSE}
garry_daemons()
t0 <- Sys.time()
cubes <- materialise(list(y2023 = year("2023"), y2025 = year("2025")))
Sys.time() - t0
```

## False colour

A principal component analysis of the 64 dimensions gives a false-colour
image. Fit it on a sample of 2025 pixels, then project every pixel of both
years with `band_project()` so the colours mean the same thing in each.

```{r pca, message = FALSE}
set.seed(1)
n <- 2e4
bb <- grid_bbox(grid)
pts <- wk::xy(
  stats::runif(n, bb[["xmin"]], bb[["xmax"]]),
  stats::runif(n, bb[["ymin"]], bb[["ymax"]]),
  crs = grid_crs(grid)
)
px <- extract_points(cubes$y2025, pts)
px <- px[stats::complete.cases(px), ]
pca <- prcomp(px)
summary(pca)$importance[, 1:3]

false_colour <- function(cube) {
  lazy_stack(lapply(1:3, function(k) {
    reduce_over(cube, band_project(pca$rotation[, k], center = pca$center),
                over = "band")
  }), along = "band")
}
```

```{r false-colour, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5.4}
op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
preview(false_colour(cubes$y2023), main = "2023")
preview(false_colour(cubes$y2025), main = "2025")
par(op)
```

The burned valley floor turns magenta in 2025. The alpine slopes shift
colour too: snow and growing seasons differ between years, and the
embeddings record that. The next section measures which shift is larger.

## Map the change

The cosine distance `1 - (a . b) / (|a| |b|)` between a pixel's 2023 and 2025
vectors measures how much its embedding changed, whatever the cause. The
vectors are not unit length, so the norms stay in the formula. Each term is
a band reduction, and garry fuses the whole expression into one kernel over
the two cubes.

```{r change}
a <- cubes$y2023
b <- cubes$y2025
dot <- reduce_over(a * b, "sum", "band")
cos_dist <- 1 - dot / sqrt(reduce_over(a * a, "sum", "band") *
                           reduce_over(b * b, "sum", "band"))
change <- collect(cos_dist)
summary(as.vector(change))
```

```{r change-map, fig.width = 6.6, fig.height = 7.2}
preview(change, col = grDevices::hcl.colors(64, "Inferno"),
        main = "Cosine distance, 2023 to 2025")
```

The burn scar stands out at roughly ten times the distance of the
unburned forest and alpine terrain around it, with the Athabasca River,
its lakes and unburned stands inside the perimeter staying dark. No
labels and no imagery went into the map: only two years of embeddings.

A threshold turns the map into an area. The value is a choice rather than
a calibration; on this map it sits in the gap between the two modes of
the distribution.

```{r area}
moved <- sum(change > 0.1, na.rm = TRUE) * 100 / 1e4 # 10 m pixels to ha
round(moved)
garry_daemons(0, 0)
```

## Notes

* **Zones.** An area across a zone boundary needs one read per zone, each
  warped onto a common grid with `align_to()`.
* **Southern zones** use the northern CRS, so their northings are
  negative. GDAL and garry handle this as any other coordinate.
* **Dimensions.** Use 64 or 128 dimensions throughout an analysis, never a
  mix. Reading all 128 (`bands = 1:128`) doubles the bytes.
* **Similarity.** For cosine similarity as a dot product, normalise the
  vectors first; their norms run from about 3.0 to 3.8.
* **Window seams.** The embeddings were computed in independent
  2048 x 2048 pixel windows, and their edges can show as faint straight
  lines in derived maps: the vertical line at x = 430,080 m in the change
  map is one (the step between neighbouring columns is about seven times
  its usual size there).
